<Base
	title="About Steven Yule"
	description="Steven Yule, chartered civil engineer in Dubai, with 16 years making digital and AI useful on live capital programmes across the GCC and the UK."
>
	<div class="page-head">
		<h1 class="page-title">About Steven Yule</h1>
	</div>

	<div class="prose reveal">
		<p>
			I am Steven Yule, a chartered civil engineer based in Dubai, and I lead
			digital, data and AI work for infrastructure at director level. For 16
			years I have worked across the Gulf and the UK on one problem, which is
			making technology useful once it meets a live capital programme.
		</p>
		<p>
			Over that time I have built and led teams covering digital strategy,
			data engineering, data science and software delivery. I have also run a
			regional digital and data practice, which meant answering for its growth
			as well as for the quality of what it delivered. Doing both puts two
			different questions to any digital idea. The engineering tells you
			whether it will survive contact with a real asset. The commercial side
			tells you whether an organisation can buy it and keep paying to run it,
			which is often the harder question.
		</p>
		<p>
			Most digital capability stalls in the same place. It is proven in a
			pilot and then treated as finished because it worked once. The harder
			question is whether an organisation can rely on it the next morning, on
			a live programme where real decisions depend on it. The gap between a
			capability that works and one that can be depended on is where I spend
			most of my time, and it is usually settled earlier than people
			expect, in the procurement and in who owns what at handover. The same
			gap appears at the top of a large owner, where each development can be
			relied on separately and the sum of them cannot.
		</p>
		<p>
			The clearest example of reading across developments is Riyadh. As
			project director with the Riyadh Infrastructure Projects Center, I led
			the first integrated infrastructure plan for the region. It brought the
			works programmes of more than 20 government and utility bodies together
			across thousands of projects. Road space and working time could then be
			booked once by agreement, instead of being fought over site by site.
			The plan is publicly credited with preventing more than 5,000 conflicts
			between their works.
		</p>
		<p>
			The work is commissioned in three ways. An <a href="/review/"
				>independent review</a
			> gives an owner a written second opinion on a decision before it is
			confirmed. Continuing advisory is for decisions that keep arriving over
			months. A non-executive or board appointment is for the challenge that
			has to be made in the room where the decision is taken. The review
			is usually where an owner starts, and all three are described under <a
				href="/work/">Work</a
			>.
		</p>
		<p>
			My background covers transport, utilities, smart cities, the built
			environment, data-centre enablement and asset-heavy capital programmes,
			in the UK and in the Gulf.
		</p>
		<p>
			I am a Fellow of the Institution of Civil Engineers (CEng FICE), an ICE
			Policy Fellow and the ICE UAE Country Representative. I am a Fellow of
			the Chartered Institution of Highways and Transportation (FCIHT), a
			member of the Institute of Asset Management (MIAM) and a Freeman of the
			Worshipful Company of Engineers. I also serve as a non-executive
			director of Thomas Telford Ltd.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="origin-note reveal">
		<h2 class="origin-note-title">About the name</h2>
		<p>
			OkArthur is named after my son Arthur, who is seven and asks more
			questions, and sharper ones, than I am always ready for. Ok, Arthur
			became the answer, and then it became the name. Taking a question
			seriously, and not settling for the defensible answer, is most of what
			good advice turns out to be.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>
